NAME
    grep - keep lines matching a regular expression

SYNOPSIS
    <input> | grep [-i] [-v] [-n] [-F] <pattern>

DESCRIPTION
    Reads standard input and writes only the lines that match <pattern>, an
    extended regular expression (the egrep dialect). A pipeline filter.

    -i  ignore case (ASCII)
    -v  invert: print the lines that do NOT match
    -n  prefix each printed line with its 1-based input line number
    -F  fixed string: match <pattern> as a literal substring, no regex

    Flags may be combined, e.g. -in, and come before the pattern.

PATTERNS
    c           the literal character c
    .           any single character
    [abc]       any one of a, b, c        [a-z] a range      [^0-9] negated
    ^   $       start / end of the line
    e*  e+  e?  zero-or-more, one-or-more, zero-or-one of e
    ab          a followed by b
    a|b         a or b
    (e)         grouping
    \c          the literal c (\. \* \[ \\ ...); \n and \t are newline and tab

    Alternation binds loosest: ^a|b$ means "starts with a" OR "ends with b",
    not ^(a|b)$. The line terminator is stripped before matching, so $ anchors
    to the end of the visible text.

    Not supported: back-references, {n,m} counted repetition, POSIX class names
    like [:alpha:], and submatch capture.

    A repeat whose body can match nothing - (a*)* or (a|)* - is REFUSED with
    "repeat of a possibly-empty expression". POSIX allows it; this engine does
    not, because an empty loop body is the one way a backtracking matcher can
    spin forever. Refusing it is what makes every accepted pattern terminate.
    Write a* instead of (a*)*.

    A bad pattern is an error, not a fallback: grep says what is wrong and
    exits rather than quietly searching for something else. Very costly
    patterns can exhaust the matcher's bounded budget on a given line; those
    lines are skipped and a one-time warning says so.

TYPING A PATTERN AT THIS SHELL
    There are no quotes yet, so a pattern is a single whitespace-free word.
    Two consequences worth knowing:

      - A pipe is only a pipeline separator when it stands alone between
        spaces, so grep a|b works as alternation. Write grep "a | b" - with
        spaces - and the shell splits it into pipeline stages instead.
      - The shell expands * and ? against the filesystem before grep sees
        them. A pattern matching no filename is passed through unchanged
        (the usual case), but in a directory where it DOES match, the
        filename arrives instead of the pattern.

EXAMPLES
    cat log.txt | grep ERROR
    cat log.txt | grep ERROR|WARN|FATAL
    cat /etc/passwd | grep ^root
    cat /etc/passwd | grep -n ^[a-z]+:
    ls /bin | grep -i cat
    cat log.txt | grep -vn WARN
    cat notes.txt | grep -F a.b          the literal text a.b

SEE ALSO
    wc, head, tail, uniq, sort
